      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by \\(12\\) points in space, we need to consider the combinatorial event of choosing \\(3\\) points out of \\(12\\). Number of ways to select \\(3\\) points from \\(12\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}3 = 220\n\\]\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that for \\(2, 3, \\ldots, n\\) points in space, the number of planes formed is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{3}\n\\]\n\nGiven this specific problem, the starting point to determine a plane is 3 points. If each 3 points give one unique plane in 12 points space, then:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ points} \\rightarrow \\binom{12}{3} = 220 \\text{ planes}\n\\]\n\nThis means we have calculated the exact scenario where each group of 3 independent points determine a plane. The answer, as explained, is:\n\n\\boxed{220}",
